---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-brevo';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-brevo');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-brevo');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is the old Brevo Shopify app the current installation path?",
    "answer": "Brevo documents Brevo PushOwl as the only supported Shopify connection and says its former app is unavailable for new stores."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a Drip event test include?",
    "answer": "Compare identified add-to-cart with checkout events and inspect the queued product block after a cart update."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does import preserve all Brevo subscription statuses?",
    "answer": "Brevo warns Shopify import may overwrite existing attributes, including subscription status. Preserve and reconcile suppression evidence before sending."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does cheaper sending guarantee cheaper recovery?",
    "answer": "No. Include enabled channel scope, setup work, ongoing operator time and net contribution from incremental orders."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare the cost of a working Shopify recovery pipeline: Drip cart-event control versus the current Brevo PushOwl connection, with an explicit migration and operating budget.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Keep or evaluate", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Use when the existing event-driven workflow can be maintained and its rendering checks pass."}, {"label": "Evaluate connection", "tool": "Brevo", "reason": "Use the supported PushOwl path after reconciling the destination account, imports and total operating cost."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>Brevo</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Shopify entry</td><td>Identified cart and checkout events</td><td>Current Brevo PushOwl app</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance focus</td><td>Queued content after item changes</td><td>Correct account, subscription status and tracker</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget scope</td><td>Current audience/channel quote and workflow upkeep</td><td>Actual app plan, channel use and migration upkeep</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Compare the supported installations</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s current Shopify guide</a> identifies Brevo PushOwl as its only supported connection; the former app is no longer available to new stores. It describes Shopify data sync, tracking and abandoned-cart messaging. Select the intended existing Brevo account during setup and confirm the offered channel plan. Do not compare a legacy standalone price with a new Shopify app quote. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Trace the event before comparing send volume</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">Drip’s event guide</a> distinguishes identified add-to-cart activity from checkout activity. Its cart block uses the latest cart event, but additions after an email is queued are excluded. Proposed exercise: add one item as a known shopper, enter checkout, add another item after queuing and inspect the actual message and return link. Record the chosen event, contact key, cart identifier, currency and timestamps. Repeat in Brevo PushOwl and compare delivered content rather than assuming identical events.</p>
<h2>Make import reconciliation a budgeted task</h2>
<p>Brevo warns in the same connection guide that importing Shopify data makes Shopify authoritative and may overwrite contact attributes, including subscription status. Save the existing suppression record before import. Compare a known unsubscribed contact, an email subscriber, an SMS-only subscriber and a customer without marketing permission after a controlled import. Explain changes before releasing messages. Activate the theme tracker and verify the destination account receives events; an imported customer list alone does not prove an operational abandoned-cart queue.</p>
<h2>Use a checkout repair matrix</h2>
<p>Start with the expensive exception: a bundle whose advertised discount is absent. Verify the cart rule before sending another code, then test that the link restores the selected variants and current price. For international shipping shock, show the actual quote and delivery terms. A high-value financing or BNPL dropout should get supported payment assistance, never a promise of approval. MOQ failures belong with a sales owner who can resolve quantity requirements.</p>
<p>For gift messages and sizing, preserve the shopper’s selection and give support enough context to help. Remove unavailable variants from recovery and use a separately permitted stock-alert path. A subscription-toggle objection needs clear purchase terms; a repeat buyer may already have an applicable benefit. Use a simple return link for a distracted mobile shopper. These are proposed content and routing cases, not claims that either system infers the abandonment reason.</p>
<h2>Calculate the discount cost before the software saving</h2>
<p>Hypothetical $638 cart at 35% gross margin has $414.70 fixed goods cost and $223.30 gross profit at full price. An 18% code lowers revenue to $523.16 and gross profit to $108.46, losing $114.84 per order recovered at that price. A separate $423 cart at 67% margin has $283.41 full-price gross profit; a 19% code leaves $203.04, a reduction of $80.37. These are scenarios, not observed average carts or recommended discount ceilings. Shipping, payment fees, returns, platform charges and labor remain excluded.</p>
<p>Build a monthly quote with reachable contacts, expected campaign and recovery sends, applicable SMS segments and any push scope. Add setup hours, template work, consent reconciliation and the person maintaining exception routes. Divide fixed monthly cost by incremental recovered orders only after a credible measurement period; at low order counts, a small subscription saving can be outweighed by one unnecessary incentive or manual incident.</p>
<h2>Move the flow, then prove the exits</h2>
<p>Inventory existing Drip and Shopify recovery automations, export exclusions and assign one owner for the cutover. Rebuild a single cart flow with controlled records first. Test a purchase while waiting, a later opt-out, a repeated checkout update and an expired or unavailable item. Require cancellation of the pending reminder and deduplication for the same cart. If SMS or push is enabled, verify separate permission and a shared contact cap; email permission does not authorize either channel.</p>
<p>Assign in-flight carts explicitly, pause the old entry point, reconcile the last sent message and enable new entry for a small eligible cohort. Keep the old configuration available for rollback, with event checkpoints that prevent replay. Broaden to browse and VIP exclusions only after the cart flow passes. Use a preselected holdout sized for the store’s volume and compare deduplicated net contribution plus operator hours. A revenue dashboard alone does not settle the Drip/Brevo decision.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
